  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nWe tackle the minimum overlap problem using a Gibbs sampler-based optimization algorithm with periodic covariance structure. The core idea is to represent the step function through latent probability variables with controllable correlations, allowing the search to navigate through high-dimensional space efficiently. Through underdamped MCMC with inverse temperature control, we enforce the solution to be mostly binary while maintaining the integral constraint.\n\nThis approach differs from previous methods by incorporating two key innovations:\na) Using periodic sinusoidal covariances as the underlying structure for the hidden Markov process\nb) Implementing an adaptive temperature annealing scheme coupled with acceptance/rejection to prevent local minima trapping\n\nWe accept solutions that strictly satisfy 0<=h<=1 exactly, not as approximations. The Gibbs sampling adaptation ensures that we explore the entire constrained space thoroughly.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nVariables:\n- h(x): step function in [0,1] for x in [0,2]\n- \u03b8(x): latent periodic covariance process driving h(x)\n- \u03b2(x): inverse temperature parameter (>=1)\n- \u03bb: variance parameter for the latent process\n\nConstraints:\n1. 0 \u2264 h_i \u2264 1 for all i\n2. \u2211_{i=1}^{n} h_i * dx = 1  (where dx=2/n, n\u224880)\n\nObjective:\nminimize C_5 = max_k \u222b h(x) \u2022 (1-h(x+k)) dx\n\nRepresentation:\nThe Gibbs sampler introduces a latent stochastic process \u03b8(x) with periodic covariance:\n  \u03b8(x) = A + B \u2022 sin(\u03c9x + \u03c6) + noise\n\nHere, h(x) is obtained by applying a sigmoid transformation:\n  h(x) = 1/(1+exp(-(\u03b8(x) - \u03bc)))   [but with training adaptively]\n\nGradient handling:\nWe compute gradients using automatic differentiation in JAX, but note the algorithm is written in pure Python or similar.\n\n3. Implementation procedure\n3.1 Initialization:\n- Generate parent representation if provided\n- Initialize \u03b8(x) based on sinusoidal periodic function\n\n3.2 Gibbs sampling core:\n- Maintain an adaptive inverse temperature \u03b2(t) that increases with time\n- At each temperature \u03b2(since_start):\n  1. Sample new \u03b8(x) from normal distribution with covariance kernel:\n      K(x,y) = exp(-|sin(\u03c9(x)-sin(\u03c9(y))||/\u03bb)\n  2. Transform \u03b8 to h via sigmoid acceptance\n  3. Project h to satisfy the integral constraint using:\n      h_corrected = min(1, max(0, h + c*(40-sum(h_gain))))\n   (with exact normalization if not integer)\n  4. Compute C_5 using np.correlate with mode='full'\n\n3.3 Optimization loop:\n- Use AdamOptimizer with adaptive learning rate\n- Track best-known solution across time\n\n3.4 Local repair function:\n- Clip h_i to [0,1]\n- Adjust sum by uniformly scaling remaining capacity\n\n3.5 Objective evaluation:\n- We carefully ensure exact [0,1] and sum normalization\ncompute_corr = lambda h: np.max(np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode=\"full\"))*(2.0/n)\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\n- Temperature profile: \u03b2_start = 0.1, \u03b2_max = 10, adaptive increase factor = 1.01\n- Learning rate = 0.05\n- Dimensionality reduction for high u = n//8\n- Noise magnitude \u03bb in [0.1,1]\n- Multiple sampling patterns: 3 sinusoid phases\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n- Primary cost: 80-point correlation = 6400 operations\n- Budget estimate: \n  eval_time = 6400 * (#samples) * (additional computation)\n  Budget_ops = 6400 * 1e6  (millions of allowed ops)\n- Time estimate: 1100 seconds / 60fps \u2248 ~18,000 evaluations\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n- We validate: (a) 0<=h[i]<=1 after repair; (b) sum(h)=40 exactly (within 1e-7)\n- We recompute the score with one full np.correlate\n- Return tuple (h_values, c5_bound, n_points)\n\nWe note that this algorithm explores the entire feasible region systematically, with the Gibbs process encouraged toward binary domains through increasing temperature. Constraint restoration is done exactly, not approximately, ensuring valid solutions.",
